@title Sale-to-Stock Flow
@subtitle How a register sale reaches the online stock count
@author Retail Platform team
@date 2026-10-08

## Sale-to-stock sequence
diagram sale sequence title="Sale-to-stock sequence"
sale: pos "Store POS" svc
sale: edge "Edge Sync Agent" svc
sale: api "Inventory API" svc
sale: ledger "Stock Ledger" store
sale: front "Storefront" svc
sale: pos -> edge : sale recorded, 2 units of SKU 48113
sale: edge -> api : POST /adjustments, batched every 5 seconds
sale: api -> ledger : append decrement
sale: ledger --> api : new on-hand count
sale: api --> edge : 202 Accepted
sale: api -> front : publish stock change

When the store loses its connection, Store POS keeps selling and Edge Sync Agent keeps the adjustments in a local queue. When the link returns, the agent sends them in order as "replay queued adjustments" to Inventory API.

!! The Stock Ledger is the only source of truth.
! The Storefront count may lag by up to 20 seconds.

## Timing budget
table = Hop,Limit; Register to edge agent,under 1 second; Agent batch interval,5 seconds; Inventory API to ledger,under 200 milliseconds; Ledger to storefront,under 10 seconds; End-to-end target,under 20 seconds for 99% of sales

## Good to know
- The local queue holds up to 4 hours of sales, which is about 3000 adjustments at a busy store.
- Adjustments carry an idempotency key, so replays never double count.
- Online orders reserve stock for 15 minutes at checkout.
